==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|fr|sacristain}}, {{uder|en|LL.|sacrista}}, from {{uder|en|la|sacer}}. See {{m|en|sacred}}, and compare {{m|en|sexton}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# The [[person]] who maintains the [[sacristy]] and the sacred objects it contains.
#* '''1793,''' [[w:Samuel Taylor Coleridge|Samuel Taylor Coleridge]], ''[[s:Christabel|Christabel]]''
#*: And hence the custom and law began
#*: That still at dawn the '''sacristan''',
#*: Who duly pulls the heavy bell,
#*: Five and forty beads must tell
#*: Between each stroke
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1879|author=F. D. Morice|title=[[s:en:Pindar (Morice)|Pindar]]|publisher=[[s:Pindar (Morice)/Chapter 3|chapter 3]]|page=32
|passage={{...|Either, as some say, at every sacrifice the priest invoked the shade of Pindar to take his share of the offerings, or, according to another version of the legend,}} every evening as the temple was closed for the night the '''sacristan''' paused: "''Pindar to supper with the god!''" he cried.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1932|author=[[w:Maurice Baring|Maurice Baring]]
|title=Friday's Business
|chapter=10|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL45537W
|passage=The church was looked after by an old '''sacristan''' who lived in a cottage on the shore of the lake.}}

